  "title": "Dopamine: The Reward Signal",
  "emoji": "⚡",
  "color": "#8B5CF6",
  "description": "Learn Dopamine: The Reward Signal in one minute.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You check your phone for likes. A tiny dopamine burst rewards you. You check again. And again.",
      "visual": "📱"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Dopamine is released in the brain's reward pathways when something feels good or exciting.",
      "visual": "🎉"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "But dopamine isn't about pleasure itself—it signals anticipation. It's the wanting, not the having.",
      "visual": "🎯"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "In Parkinson's disease, dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra die, causing movement problems.",
      "visual": "🤲"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Too much dopamine activity is linked to schizophrenia. Too little, to depression and apathy.",
      "visual": "⚖️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Dopamine doesn't create happiness. It creates drive.",
      "visual": "🚀"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What does dopamine primarily signal?",
    "options": [
      "Anticipation and motivation",
      "Pain relief",
      "Sleep onset"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
